WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:04.110
Most of us know

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.330
what a dragon sounds like,

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.810
but they don't actually exist.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:12.243
And none can stop me!

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.340
So, how does Hollywood know

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.144
how to make them sound?

00:00:18.144 --> 00:00:19.331
Dracarys!

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.410
Just like visual artists, sound designers

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.330
take inspiration from
the world around them.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.400
The sounds for creatures
like dragons, dinosaurs,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.453
and even Chewbacca are
based on real-world animals.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:41.190
A sound designer may
use sounds from lions,

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:44.250
elephants, cats, and even tortoises

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.260
to create the sound for just one dragon.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.880
They'll layer sounds
together to create the sound

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.606
of a non-existent creature.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.380
So, my dogs are in the second episode

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:57.350
of season five.

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.660
There's a scene that's very intimate

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.790
and you hear these like
beautiful little nasal whistles.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.403
Okay, that was my dog, Angel.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.900
She's in a lot of Drogon's stuff

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.300
and obviously in the wolves.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.603
So, I took that sound and put it in there.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:19.870
Paula Fairfield is a sound designer

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.250
for "Game of Thrones."

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:25.000
Over the last five seasons,
Paula has carefully crafted

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.610
the sounds of Drogon,
Rhaegal, and Viserion.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:31.530
She even used the vocal screams of fans

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:35.480
to create the sound generated
by Viserion's icy breath

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:37.243
in the season seven finale.

00:01:40.406 --> 00:01:41.880
The final sound design

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:43.760
of a scene is complex.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.670
It masks the individual
sound effects so the viewer

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.770
can't identify the sources.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:51.200
But, we can build our
own simplified version

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.800
of a dragon's sound.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.870
Combine some alligator,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.030
a little elephant,

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.210
horse,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.410
add a pig

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:00.353
and you've got the sound of a dragon.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.095
But, these are complex creatures.

00:02:09.095 --> 00:02:10.170
Dragons do more

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.480
than just roar and breathe fire.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.980
Sound designers also use
sound to craft a performance

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:16.920
for the dragon.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.840
Just like Daenerys, Drogon
needs to act and perform.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.880
Sound designers take
emotional cues from animals

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.220
to influence the sounds they create.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:27.750
I was really proud of the fact

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:30.220
that you can always hear
from the time he was,

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.840
you know, a toddler to now you can hear

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.821
the essence of him in his voice.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:35.800
You recognize him as Drogon.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.310
And I see them as like your puppy dog.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.990
I mean, we have grown up
with these puppy dragons

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:42.440
in our world, right?

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.880
And, I think that's why there
is so much love for them.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.880
I've tried to bring my
love of that into the show.

00:02:51.049 --> 00:02:53.260
Each dragon is unique and sound designers

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.520
are always trying something new.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.040
In "The Hobbit," director Peter Jackson

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:01.120
used Benedict Cumberbatch's
original performance

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.850
as Smaug to influence the
sound design of the dragon.

00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:08.500
Many of the pauses, hisses,
and vocal affectations

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.471
came from Cumberbatch.

00:03:10.471 --> 00:03:13.550
So, tell me, thief,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.533
how do you choose to die?

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.420
These details help make Smaug

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.140
incredibly impactful and memorable.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.490
And, in the "How to Train
Your Dragon" series,

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:28.000
sound designer Randy Thom
used different types of sounds

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.870
for each species of dragon

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:32.573
giving each type of creature
their own unique sound.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:45.080
Dragons and other fantastical creatures

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.130
add a larger-than-life element to stories.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:51.040
The most memorable dragons
leave space for the sound

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:53.860
to have an emotional effect on the viewer.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:56.430
Without the incredible sound design,

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:59.270
"Game of Thrones" and
"How To Train Your Dragon"

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.023
may not be as memorable.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.500
They're beautiful aren't they?

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.160
Wasn't the word I was thinking of,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.589
but,

00:04:08.589 --> 00:04:10.089
but yes, they are.

